WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.806
Automation expands the places secrets can appear.

2
00:00:02.806 --> 00:00:13.116
CI/CD systems, deployment scripts, container builds, infrastructure-as-
code pipelines, notebooks, data workflows, scheduled jobs, and test
environments may all need credentials.

3
00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:21.990
The risk is that a secret used for one short operation gets copied into
logs, images, caches, artifacts, debug output, or long-lived variables
with broad access.

4
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:27.733
Automation should retrieve secrets securely and keep them out of durable
artifacts whenever possible.

5
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:30.343
Build-time and runtime secrets are different.

6
00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:35.237
A build may need a temporary credential to fetch a private dependency or
sign an artifact.

7
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:39.609
A runtime service may need a database password or API token while serving
users.

8
00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:44.568
Secrets used during builds should not be baked into container images or
compiled outputs.

9
00:00:44.568 --> 00:00:51.420
Container images may be stored, pulled, scanned, cached, shared, and
reused well beyond the intended runtime environment.

10
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.291
CI/CD secret variables should be scoped carefully.

11
00:00:54.291 --> 00:00:58.663
Not every branch, pull request, or job should receive production
credentials.

12
00:00:58.663 --> 00:01:02.709
Deployment credentials should be separated by environment and purpose.

13
00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:10.148
Infrastructure-as-code should reference secrets without embedding secret
values directly in repository files or generated plans.

14
00:01:10.148 --> 00:01:16.999
Test environments should use test secrets with limited access rather than
production credentials copied for convenience.

15
00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:20.914
Notebooks and data workflows deserve the same discipline as services.

16
00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:24.503
Exploratory Python code often grows into scheduled automation.

17
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:30.637
A token pasted into a notebook can end up in version history, shared
storage, output cells, or exported reports.

18
00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:40.360
The safe pattern is to use approved secret retrieval, keep outputs clean,
limit access, and treat automation as production-like when it touches real
systems or sensitive data.
